    "q-332828": {
      "dom_id": "step-q332828",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)=-{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)-{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{3{x}_{1}(t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=3L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+4L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-3L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the results\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\}=\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16},\n$$\n\nwe can write our system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)=3{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{4s}{{s}^{2}+16} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)=-{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)-\\frac{12}{{s}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
